weave-knee trial v1
fanout: 12
seed: 2
weaver: claude-sonnet-5
judge: claude-sonnet-5
quality: 2
narrative:
  At the center of Feltol sits Pammir, the scheduler, which refuses new writes while its own sweep is running and keeps only digests of what it carries, never the bodies; it acts only once three of its peers countersign, and when it does act it signals backpressure to Felvar, the gate. Felvar answers reads from its newest snapshot only, evicts anything left unclaimed after six ticks, and retries a failed handoff at most twice before parking it; under that backpressure, Felvar delegates its overflow to Pamkel, the index. Pamkel, like Pammir, keeps digests of what it carries rather than bodies, answers reads from its newest snapshot only, and refuses new writes while its sweep is running; once its ledger is sealed, Pamkel hands it to Keldor, the vault. Keldor retries a failed handoff at most twice before parking it, evicts anything left unclaimed after nine ticks, and answers reads from its newest snapshot only; Keldor publishes its sweep verdicts to Dorbra, the beacon. Dorbra refuses new writes while its sweep is running, retries a failed handoff at most three times before parking it, and keeps digests of what it carries, never the bodies; Dorbra streams its evictions to Gritol, the registry. Gritol keeps digests rather than bodies, evicts anything left unclaimed after nine ticks, and answers reads from its newest snapshot only; Gritol reports missed heartbeats to Zinsax, the ledger. Zinsax evicts anything left unclaimed after ten ticks, retries a failed handoff at most once before parking it, and refuses new writes while its sweep is running; Zinsax signals backpressure to Feldor, the relay. Feldor evicts anything left unclaimed after nine ticks, refuses new writes while its sweep is running, and keeps digests of what it carries, never the bodies; under that backpressure, Feldor delegates its overflow to Varvar, the archive. Varvar acts only once four of its peers countersign, evicts anything left unclaimed after ten ticks, and refuses new writes while its sweep is running; once its ledger is sealed, Varvar hands it to Ruvruv, the courier. Ruvruv evicts anything left unclaimed after eight ticks, acts only once three of its peers countersign, and answers reads from its newest snapshot only; Ruvruv publishes its sweep verdicts to Tolbra, the broker. Tolbra keeps digests of what it carries, never the bodies, evicts anything left unclaimed after twelve ticks, and retries a failed handoff at most three times before parking it; Tolbra streams its evictions to Tolost, the sampler. Tolost evicts anything left unclaimed after five ticks, answers reads from its newest snapshot only, and refuses new writes while its sweep is running; and Tolost closes the circuit by reporting missed heartbeats back to Pammir, the scheduler that began it.

  Taken together, the twelve modules form a single ring rather than a hierarchy: each hands something forward, whether overflow, a sealed ledger, sweep verdicts, evictions, or a missed-heartbeat report, to exactly one downstream module, and receives from exactly one upstream module in turn. Backpressure travels twice around this ring: once from Pammir to Felvar and once from Zinsax to Feldor, each followed immediately by an overflow delegation, Felvar to Pamkel and Feldor to Varvar respectively. Sealed ledgers move twice as well, from Pamkel to Keldor and from Varvar to Ruvruv. Sweep verdicts move twice, from Keldor to Dorbra and from Ruvruv to Tolbra. Evictions stream twice, from Dorbra to Gritol and from Tolbra to Tolost. And missed heartbeats are reported twice, from Gritol to Zinsax and from Tolost back to Pammir, the two reports that, together with the two backpressure signals, mark the ring's quarter-turns.
